ounseling in patients using these traditional medicines. Failure to heed these warnings could lead to reduced drug efficacy or unintended physiological effects. [Data: Relationships (28199, 43297, 27355, 33076); Entities (12743, 12761, 10731)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Contraindications and safety considerations are specified\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Safety guidance is embedded in several structured relationships. For instance, 参茸鹿胎膏 should not be used during 感冒 (common cold), as tonifying agents may 'trap pathogenic factors' according to TCM theory. Additionally, 参茸珍宝 requires avoidance of 油腻食物 (greasy foods), likely to prevent digestive burden or interference with absorption. There are also classical herb-herb incompatibilities, such as 藜芦 (Veratrum) being contraindicated with 补中益气 (a qi-tonifying formula) and 人参 (ginseng), based on the 'Eighteen Incompatible Pairings' in TCM. Modern research further supports that 五灵脂 (Wu Ling Zhi) can degrade active components in 人参, reducing efficacy. These contraindications reflect both traditional doctrine and emerging pharmacological evidence. [Data: Relationships (5858, 31949, 50583, 28113); Entities (4122, 12788, 12298)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Metabolic interactions with cytochrome P450 enzymes are noted\",\n            \"explanation\": \"南沙参 (Southern Glehnia Root) is associated with modulation of CYP2C9, a key drug-metabolizing enzyme. Evidence indicates that herbal injections containing 南沙参 (as part of complex formulas like 热毒宁注射液) can induce or inhibit various CYP enzymes, potentially altering the metabolism of co-administered drugs such as 华法林 (warfarin). This raises concerns about herb-drug interactions in patients on narrow-therapeutic-index medications. Although 南沙参 itself appears only once in the entity list, its inclusion signals that even less prominent herbs in this community may carry significant pharmacokinetic interaction risks. [Data: Entitie